Schemas
description: $defs onto and fragment, and the all-colon project-scope spelling
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: bullets>
  - '**`$defs: onto`** (`format: x-yamlover-onto`) - an entity of an ontology: an optional `color`, optionally sub-ontos; otherwise open.'
  - '**`$defs: fragment`** (`format: x-yamlover-fragment`) - an omni: the self-value carries the fragment''s principal content (the quoted text), the selector fields beside it (`type, prefix, suffix, start, end, page, x, y, w, h, n, s, e, w`), an optional binary `image` crop, `created` - and the rest is open (`description`, a keyless pointer as the link, ...).'
- >
  There is **no `$defs: annotation`** - an application is a bookmark edge, and edges carry no
  data ([applications](*::annotations:applications)).
- >
  Both are reached as `*::yamlover:$defs:<name>` (the yamlover project is grafted at every served
  root's self-import key `yamlover`). Note the **all-colon** spelling: a project scope `::` must
  be followed by colon steps - `*::yamlover/$defs/onto` parses the `/`-path as a link *authority*
  and does not resolve, whereas `*yamlover/$defs/onto` (no `::`) is a current-scope slash path
  that does. Server-written ontos use the colon form.
